  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "It's morning in Lyova's flat, and Barinov has made breakfast for everyone. Lyova suspects it's his way of apologising. Listen in Russian first.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Доброе утро, Виктор.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "mama",
   "t": "Доброе утро.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "mama",
   "t": "Виктор приготовил такой омлет.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Это типа извинения?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Это типа омлет.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Ешь давай.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Я опоздаю на работу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "mama",
   "t": "Да, кушайте быстрее.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Ладно, я съем это.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Это очень вкусно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "mama",
   "t": "Нам пора идти.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Спасибо за завтрак.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Slowly, around the breakfast table.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Доброе утро, Виктор.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good morning, Viktor.' 'Utro' is a neuter word, so 'dobroe', 'good', ends in 'oe'. Lyova's mother calls him by his first name, as an equal.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mama",
   "t": "Доброе утро.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good morning.' It's the same greeting in two words. The mother says it to the room.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mama",
   "t": "Виктор приготовил такой омлет.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Viktor prepared such an omelet.' 'Such an omelette' is masculine, because 'omlet' is. The word is borrowed from French.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Это типа извинения?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Is this a kind of apology?' 'Tipa' is slang for 'sort of', and it's the kind of word teenagers use. 'Izvineniya' is 'apologies', always plural.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Это типа омлет.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'This is a kind of omelet.' Barinov copies 'tipa' back, to avoid the subject. It's a neat way to say nothing.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Ешь давай.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Come on, eat.' 'Esh'' is the order 'eat!', for a friend. 'Davay' tacked on at the end makes it a nudge.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Я опоздаю на работу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I will be late for work.' 'Opozdayu' is a single word for 'I will be late'. 'Na rabotu' is the direction phrase.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mama",
   "t": "Да, кушайте быстрее.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, eat faster.' 'Kushayte' is a cosy 'eat', with the 'te' that makes it polite and plural. 'Bystree' is 'faster'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Ладно, я съем это.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Alright, I will eat this.' 'I will eat up' starts with 's', which means 'finish'. 'Ladno' is the shrug.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Это очень вкусно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'This is very delicious.' Without a verb, 'eto ochen vkusno'. It's high praise from a chef.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mama",
   "t": "Нам пора идти.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'It is time for us to go.' 'Nam pora' is 'to us time'. The mother says it to herself and the men.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Спасибо за завтрак.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Thank you for the breakfast.' 'Thanks for' is followed by the thing thanked. 'Breakfast' is the same as in English, nearly. Now the breakfast scene, uncut.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Доброе утро, Виктор.",
